    <h1 class="article-title">Nasdaq-100 Historical Constituents:<br>Every Change, <em>2007–2026</em></h1>
    <p class="article-subtitle">272 companies have passed through the Nasdaq-100 since 2007 — roughly 12 changes per year. The full recent change table is below, along with the reasons assembling the complete record is harder than any single list suggests.</p>
    <div class="article-tags">
      <span class="article-tag">Nasdaq-100 constituents</span>
      <span class="article-tag">index changes</span>
      <span class="article-tag">historical components</span>
      <span class="article-tag">point-in-time data</span>
    </div>
    <p class="article-date" style="margin-top: 20px;">August 2026</p>
  </div>
</header>


<div class="article-layout">

  
  <article class="prose">

    <p>If you are looking for the historical membership of the Nasdaq-100, here is the shape of the record. Between February 2007 and August 2026, <strong>272 distinct tickers</strong> held membership in the index. <strong>102 are members today</strong> (the count exceeds 100 because of dual share classes and recent spin-offs); <strong>170 have left</strong> — acquired, delisted, transferred to the NYSE, or dropped for falling below weight requirements. Across those 19.7 years the index recorded <strong>226 constituent changes</strong>, an average of just under 12 per year, with individual years ranging from 7 changes (2010, 2014, 2024) to 21 (2015).</p>

    <p>The turnover mechanism itself changed in June 2026: after decades of annual December reconstitution plus ad-hoc replacements, Nasdaq moved the index to a <strong>regular quarterly rebalancing schedule</strong>. The June 2026 rebalance replaced five names at once. If your mental model of NDX turnover is "one December shuffle per year," it is now out of date.</p>

