    <h2 id="reconstitution-faq">Nasdaq-100 Additions, Deletions, and Reconstitution — FAQ</h2>

    <h3>What is the official Nasdaq-100 reconstitution methodology?</h3>
    <p>Nasdaq re-ranks the index annually each December, based on market capitalization as of the last trading day in October (with a buffer rule so existing members already ranked in roughly the top 100+ by size aren't dropped over minor rank shuffling). As of June 2026, Nasdaq added quarterly evaluations on top of the annual reconstitution — so treating this as a once-a-year event, which most explainers still do, is now out of date. Nasdaq publishes the authoritative methodology document itself; this page tracks what actually changed on each date, cross-referenced against it.</p>

    <h3>How do Nasdaq-100 additions and deletions actually work?</h3>
    <p>Only about half of all changes are the scheduled reconstitution described above. The rest happen year-round: a company falls below the minimum weighting threshold, gets acquired or taken private, spins off a division, or transfers its primary listing — any of which can trigger an addition or deletion on an unscheduled date. That's the split in the table above: 123 of 226 recorded changes were scheduled reconstitution; the other ~45% happened on arbitrary dates throughout the year.</p>

    <h3>How many changes does the Nasdaq-100 make per year?</h3>
    <p>Roughly 12 per year on average since 2007, ranging from as few as 7 (2010) to as many as 21 (2015) — index turnover tracks the broader market cycle, with more churn during periods of heavy M&amp;A activity or sharp sector rotation (e.g. the 2015 spike, and the tech/biotech volatility of recent years).</p>

